WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:03.069
-Before I got hired as a writer,

00:00:03.102 --> 00:00:05.505
I was doing stand-up
in New York.

00:00:05.538 --> 00:00:07.907
I had just headlined
my first club, ever.

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:09.175 line:0%
I was only able to headline

00:00:09.208 --> 00:00:10.443 line:0%
'cause I was on that show
"Best Week Ever."

00:00:10.476 --> 00:00:11.444 line:0%
You remember that?
We'd be in front

00:00:11.477 --> 00:00:12.945
of colored pieces of paper.

00:00:12.978 --> 00:00:14.847
I remember this girl didn't
wanna talk to me at a bar

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:17.116
and I was on the eL train home
and I was like, "Well,

00:00:17.149 --> 00:00:19.018
at least I'm on TV tonight."
[ Laughter ]

00:00:19.051 --> 00:00:21.754
I don't know if that's a good
attitude in life to have.

00:00:21.787 --> 00:00:23.156
[ Laughing ]

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:29.996
It was the summer of 2008.

00:00:30.029 --> 00:00:35.134
I got a call from what was then
called Endeavor, now called WME.

00:00:35.167 --> 00:00:38.004
"Saturday Night Live is having
their studio tests on Thursday.

00:00:38.037 --> 00:00:39.872
They would like you
to audition."

00:00:39.905 --> 00:00:41.007
This was August 5th, Tuesday.

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:42.909
August 7th was Thursday.

00:00:42.942 --> 00:00:45.778
I said, "Yes, okay."

00:00:45.811 --> 00:00:48.181
I remember thinking,
"I'm not going to get it, but,

00:00:48.214 --> 00:00:51.751
I'll go and I'll do my favorite
like four minutes of stand-up."

00:00:51.784 --> 00:00:52.985 line:0%
So we audition downstairs

00:00:53.018 --> 00:00:55.288 line:0%
in what was
Conan's studio, on 6.

00:00:55.321 --> 00:00:57.156 line:0%
And I saw Lorne Michaels
right away.

00:00:57.189 --> 00:00:59.025
He was in the hallway
and he was on the phone.

00:00:59.058 --> 00:01:02.295
I went into the waiting room
of everyone waiting to audition.

00:01:02.328 --> 00:01:04.230
It was Nick Kroll,
T.J. Miller,

00:01:04.263 --> 00:01:06.232
Ellie Kemper,
Donald Glover,

00:01:06.265 --> 00:01:08.034
Bobby Morning.
-This smells like peppa

00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:09.268
up in here.

00:01:09.301 --> 00:01:10.303
-Guess who got it.

00:01:10.336 --> 00:01:11.370
Everyone had been saying like,

00:01:11.403 --> 00:01:12.738
"They don't laugh."

00:01:12.771 --> 00:01:14.240
So I went out
and I did like four minutes

00:01:14.273 --> 00:01:15.508
and I heard laughing.

00:01:15.541 --> 00:01:17.043 line:0%
Here are some
of my favorite people

00:01:17.076 --> 00:01:19.078 line:0%
that appear on "Law &amp; Order."

00:01:19.111 --> 00:01:21.147 line:0%
[ New York accent ]
Upset? Nah.

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:23.883 line:0%
She was smilin'.

00:01:23.916 --> 00:01:25.418
Guy she was with didn't look

00:01:25.451 --> 00:01:26.886
too happy, though.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:26.919 --> 00:01:27.887
Thank you very much.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:29.288
[ Applause ]

00:01:29.321 --> 00:01:31.290
I went home that weekend
to Chicago

00:01:31.323 --> 00:01:34.894
and I was at a restaurant
and a 212 number called

00:01:34.927 --> 00:01:36.329
and I told my mom to shut up!

00:01:36.362 --> 00:01:37.663
And -- [ Laughs ]

00:01:37.696 --> 00:01:39.065
And I picked it up
and it was Seth Meyers

00:01:39.098 --> 00:01:40.500
and he said, "We wanna offer you

00:01:40.533 --> 00:01:42.435
a writing job on the show."
I said, "Yes."

00:01:42.468 --> 00:01:43.803
He goes, "I wanna be clear.

00:01:43.836 --> 00:01:46.139
Like this is not the cast."

00:01:46.172 --> 00:01:47.473
And I was like,
"I wanna be clear.

00:01:47.506 --> 00:01:50.143
I would never think
I'd be in the cast."

00:01:50.176 --> 00:01:51.677
Sometimes you're hired here

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:55.581 line:0%
and you won't meet Lorne
for months, sometimes a year.

00:01:55.614 --> 00:01:57.116 line:0%
Bobby Moynihan was hired
as the cast.

00:01:57.149 --> 00:01:58.251 line:0%
-I was the only writer hired

00:01:58.284 --> 00:01:59.819 line:0%
and I was
in Steve Higgins' office.

00:01:59.852 --> 00:02:01.387 line:0%
So Steve said,
"Hey, Lorne's here.

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:02.588 line:0%
He wants to talk to you."

00:02:02.621 --> 00:02:04.323 line:0%
I remember I was
in an orange T-shirt

00:02:04.356 --> 00:02:06.893
and I said, "Am I underdressed?"

00:02:06.926 --> 00:02:08.661
And Steve said, "No."

00:02:08.694 --> 00:02:11.197
[ Laughing ] I walked
down the hall and I went in.

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:13.032
I said, "Hi," you know,
and he said, "Hi."

00:02:13.065 --> 00:02:15.201
He said, "You're from Chicago?"

00:02:15.234 --> 00:02:16.402
And I said, "Yeah,"
and he goes,

00:02:16.435 --> 00:02:18.471
"Now, you're here."
And he said,

00:02:18.504 --> 00:02:19.672
"And your family's in Chicago?"

00:02:19.705 --> 00:02:21.174
And I said, "Yeah.
I'm the third

00:02:21.207 --> 00:02:22.375
of four.

00:02:22.408 --> 00:02:24.377
I have two older siblings."

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:26.879
And he said,
"I know everything about you."

00:02:26.912 --> 00:02:28.915
I think he meant,
"I have met your kind

00:02:28.948 --> 00:02:30.483
[ Laughing ] many times."

00:02:30.516 --> 00:02:33.352
The first show week was
the first time that Tina Fey

00:02:33.385 --> 00:02:34.453 line:0%
played Sarah Palin.
-Live from New York,

00:02:34.486 --> 00:02:35.788 line:0%
it's Saturday Night!

00:02:35.821 --> 00:02:38.524 line:0%
-That show was hosted
by Michael Phelps.

00:02:38.557 --> 00:02:40.626
And I was walking out of pitch
and Andy Samberg was like,

00:02:40.659 --> 00:02:43.496
"I wanna do that
Mark Spitz thing."

00:02:43.529 --> 00:02:45.264
And I was like, "Yeah."

00:02:45.297 --> 00:02:47.600 line:0%
There was a Mark Spitz
talk show.

00:02:47.633 --> 00:02:49.702 line:0%
I said, "I thought
maybe his bandleader

00:02:49.735 --> 00:02:52.104
could be Eliot Spitzer
and the Spitzerfrenics,"

00:02:52.137 --> 00:02:54.974 line:0%
and Bill Hader walked up and was
like, "I do Eliot Spitzer."

00:02:55.007 --> 00:02:57.944 line:0%
This sounds like a movie
where people meet too easily.

00:02:57.977 --> 00:02:59.946
I was really scared
that writing night,

00:02:59.979 --> 00:03:04.150
but I'd never laughed so hard
in my life, writing with them.

00:03:04.183 --> 00:03:05.918 line:0%
Then I went back
to Greenpoint, Brooklyn,

00:03:05.951 --> 00:03:07.453
at like 5:00 in the morning

00:03:07.486 --> 00:03:11.157
and I woke up at 1:00 and the
read-through was at 3:00.

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:13.826
I took a taxi 'cause I was like,
"I gotta get there on time,"

00:03:13.859 --> 00:03:15.695
and then the bridge
between Greenpoint

00:03:15.728 --> 00:03:17.230
and Long Island City went up.

00:03:17.263 --> 00:03:19.031
I was like, "Oh, my God.
I'm gonna be late."

00:03:19.064 --> 00:03:20.399
I got into Midtown.

00:03:20.432 --> 00:03:21.934
It was like 2:50.

00:03:21.967 --> 00:03:23.102
Read-through starts at 3:00.

00:03:23.135 --> 00:03:24.437
I got out
[ Laughing ] of the taxi

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:26.339
and I sprinted
down Fifth Avenue.

00:03:26.372 --> 00:03:28.841
I got there and I said,
"Am I late?"

00:03:28.874 --> 00:03:31.944
And they said, "Lorne's not here
and read-through

00:03:31.977 --> 00:03:34.280
[ Laughing ] probably won't
start for another two hours."

00:03:34.313 --> 00:03:36.415
That was the beginning of

00:03:36.448 --> 00:03:37.783
a new relationship with time.

00:03:37.816 --> 00:03:40.720 line:0%
-Activia Testimonial take 1.
[ Clack ]

00:03:40.753 --> 00:03:42.855 line:0%
-My first season was really
fun because Simon Rich,

00:03:42.888 --> 00:03:44.724 line:0%
Marika Sawyer, and I
worked together a lot.

00:03:44.757 --> 00:03:47.193
I was lucky to have
a lot of stuff in the show,

00:03:47.226 --> 00:03:49.328
so I was around Lorne
a good deal.

00:03:49.361 --> 00:03:52.398
And then I went to Chicago
that summer

00:03:52.431 --> 00:03:55.001
to scout for people
to be on the show.

00:03:55.034 --> 00:03:58.070
This trip, by the way, was very
flattering to be asked to be on.

00:03:58.103 --> 00:04:00.573
We did not fly regular airline.

00:04:00.606 --> 00:04:03.009
[ Laughs ] That was like being
in a car in the sky

00:04:03.042 --> 00:04:04.610
and everyone was acting
like it is normal.

00:04:04.643 --> 00:04:05.878
We're flying into Chicago

00:04:05.911 --> 00:04:07.613
and I was looking
at the skyline.

00:04:07.646 --> 00:04:09.215
Steve Higgins started narrating

00:04:09.248 --> 00:04:10.683
what I was thinking, which was,

00:04:10.716 --> 00:04:12.151
[ Changes accent ]
"You mother[bleep]

00:04:12.184 --> 00:04:13.552
You said I wouldn't be nothin'.

00:04:13.585 --> 00:04:15.688
Now, I'm landin'
in a private plane.

00:04:15.721 --> 00:04:17.623
You [bleep] ass[bleep],
look at me now.

00:04:17.656 --> 00:04:19.191
I'm with Lorne Michaels."

00:04:19.224 --> 00:04:21.294
Going to Chicago,
where I am from,

00:04:21.327 --> 00:04:23.596
where I lived, in the city,
not far from Wrigley Field,

00:04:23.629 --> 00:04:25.431
with Lorne,
it was like I was going

00:04:25.464 --> 00:04:26.465
to Chicago for the first time.

00:04:26.498 --> 00:04:27.800
We went to a Cubs game.

00:04:27.833 --> 00:04:29.402
We get into Wrigley Field
and Lorne goes,

00:04:29.435 --> 00:04:31.003
"Let's get hats"
[ Laughing ] and we go

00:04:31.036 --> 00:04:32.338
and we've all buy Cubs hats.

00:04:32.371 --> 00:04:33.906
He bought me a Cubs hat,
which I still have.

00:04:33.939 --> 00:04:36.642
Then, the second season
was exciting.

00:04:36.675 --> 00:04:38.511
It was still just exciting
to be a writer

00:04:38.544 --> 00:04:41.380
and then Lorne came
up to me and he said,

00:04:41.413 --> 00:04:43.015
"I want you to do something
on 'Update.'"

00:04:43.048 --> 00:04:44.684
Right before I went out there,
Lorne walked up to me.

00:04:44.717 --> 00:04:46.519
He went, "Look in the lens

00:04:46.552 --> 00:04:47.987
and let your face relax."
[ Laughs ]

00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:50.489 line:0%
-Here, now, with a special
editorial on the subject,

00:04:50.522 --> 00:04:52.458 line:0%
is one of our writers,
John Mulaney.

00:04:52.491 --> 00:04:54.360 line:0%
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:04:54.393 --> 00:04:57.296 line:0%
-Thank you, Seth.

00:04:57.329 --> 00:04:58.364 line:0%
Thank you.

00:04:58.397 --> 00:04:59.598
I always felt

00:04:59.631 --> 00:05:01.534
he enjoyed the work I was doing

00:05:01.567 --> 00:05:03.269
and there was a point
where I had been here

00:05:03.302 --> 00:05:05.771
like three and a half,
four years

00:05:05.804 --> 00:05:07.907
and I was getting
a little cranky.

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:09.909 line:0%
I remember we had this sketch
called the "Kissing Family"

00:05:09.942 --> 00:05:11.977 line:0%
and we had one set
at an airport.

00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:13.779
I was mad about something
that week.

00:05:13.812 --> 00:05:15.915
We're in the meeting on Tuesday
and I said, We're working

00:05:15.948 --> 00:05:17.650
on a 'Kissing Family'
with Bill and Fred,"

00:05:17.683 --> 00:05:19.085
and he goes, "Where is it set?"
I was like,

00:05:19.118 --> 00:05:20.753
"They're saying goodbye
at an airport."

00:05:20.786 --> 00:05:22.688
And he said, "You can't
have it at the airport.

00:05:22.721 --> 00:05:24.223
They have the ropes,
the velvet ropes,"

00:05:24.256 --> 00:05:27.093
and I don't think he was
describing a commercial airport.

00:05:27.126 --> 00:05:28.527
And I said, "Where do you
want me to set it?

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:30.029
I'll set it
wherever you want it."

00:05:30.062 --> 00:05:31.864
And he was like,
"It can be an airport."

00:05:31.897 --> 00:05:33.566
I go, "Just tell me
where you want it set

00:05:33.599 --> 00:05:34.800
and I'll put it in there.

00:05:34.833 --> 00:05:36.569
Anywhere.
It's the Kissing Family.

00:05:36.602 --> 00:05:38.170
It can go anywhere."

00:05:38.203 --> 00:05:40.673
And -- [ Laughing ]

00:05:40.706 --> 00:05:42.875
I was a young man.
I was a --

00:05:42.908 --> 00:05:44.577
I didn't know how
to deal with anger.

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:46.846
So what he would do is
he would tell me a story.

00:05:46.879 --> 00:05:49.348
In the story was the lesson
you were supposed to know.

00:05:49.381 --> 00:05:51.250
There was a Christmas
tree lighting.

00:05:51.283 --> 00:05:52.918
So he walks up to Simon and I.

00:05:52.951 --> 00:05:55.121
We were like, "Johnny Carson
used to be on the sixth floor?"

00:05:55.154 --> 00:05:56.255
And he said, "Yeah.

00:05:56.288 --> 00:05:58.057
And then he moved to L.A.

00:05:58.090 --> 00:05:59.625
and Ed McMahon, of course,

00:05:59.658 --> 00:06:01.060
suddenly wants a new deal.

00:06:01.093 --> 00:06:03.195
So he signs with the
William Morris Agency.

00:06:03.228 --> 00:06:04.630
Ed's agents call Johnny Carson.

00:06:04.663 --> 00:06:06.065
They go,
'He wants a higher salary

00:06:06.098 --> 00:06:08.134
and to have a higher title
on the show.'

00:06:08.167 --> 00:06:09.368
Johnny Carson says, 'Fine.

00:06:09.401 --> 00:06:10.369
We'll find
a different announcer.'

00:06:10.402 --> 00:06:11.737
Ed McMahon freaked out.

00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:14.273
He had to call Johnny Carson
and apologize.

00:06:14.306 --> 00:06:17.810
Because it's that thing of when

00:06:17.843 --> 00:06:20.513
you have a personal
relationship with someone

00:06:20.546 --> 00:06:22.281
and then representatives
get involved

00:06:22.314 --> 00:06:25.451
and then that person doesn't
really wanna deal with you."

00:06:25.484 --> 00:06:27.720
And he walked away.
And Simon went,

00:06:27.753 --> 00:06:30.156
"That story was not
about Johnny Carson."

00:06:30.189 --> 00:06:31.557
[ Laughter ]

00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:35.561
I had some like opportunities
outside the show.

00:06:35.594 --> 00:06:36.629
I don't know.

00:06:36.662 --> 00:06:38.464 line:0%
I was getting really exhausted.

00:06:38.497 --> 00:06:41.600 line:0%
I left after four full years.

00:06:41.633 --> 00:06:44.336
My last episode was
Kristen Wiig's last episode

00:06:44.369 --> 00:06:46.005
and it was Andy's last episode

00:06:46.038 --> 00:06:48.240
and it was my last episode,
but no one knew that

00:06:48.273 --> 00:06:49.442
'cause I didn't tell anyone.

00:06:49.475 --> 00:06:51.076 line:0%
The last sketch,
she's like a rainbow

00:06:51.109 --> 00:06:53.946 line:0%
and "Ruby Tuesday" played
and everyone danced with Wiig.

00:06:53.979 --> 00:06:56.115 line:0%
I was standing right there.
I was with my now-wife.

00:06:56.148 --> 00:06:57.383
I remember just like crying,

00:06:57.416 --> 00:06:59.318
like really like
[ Laughing ] emotional.

00:06:59.351 --> 00:07:00.853
But not crying like cathartic.

00:07:00.886 --> 00:07:03.289
Crying like a maniac
who's been awake for four years.

00:07:03.322 --> 00:07:06.525
I was scared. I was scared
of what I was doing.

00:07:06.558 --> 00:07:09.428
I met with Lorne in July
and said I wasn't going

00:07:09.461 --> 00:07:13.265
to return, but that I wanted
to develop a show with him.

00:07:13.298 --> 00:07:15.901
And he said, "That's great.

00:07:15.934 --> 00:07:17.970
I just need you
for these weeks."

00:07:18.003 --> 00:07:19.171
[ Laughing ]

00:07:19.204 --> 00:07:20.940
And he pointed
to all the weeks of the show.

00:07:20.973 --> 00:07:23.943
I think I needed to draw
some line in the sand.

00:07:23.976 --> 00:07:26.779
Not that I was unhappy,
but, "I've done a lot of work

00:07:26.812 --> 00:07:28.080
and it is time
for me to move on

00:07:28.113 --> 00:07:30.349
and, therefore,
I do not work here."

00:07:30.382 --> 00:07:32.151 line:0%
At the beginning
of the relationship,

00:07:32.184 --> 00:07:33.552 line:0%
when it's going well,

00:07:33.585 --> 00:07:36.021 line:0%
so you decide to meet
each other's parents.

00:07:36.054 --> 00:07:37.022 line:0%
[ Laughter ]

00:07:37.055 --> 00:07:38.023 line:0%
-"Mulaney."

00:07:38.056 --> 00:07:39.024 line:0%
Coming this fall.

00:07:39.057 --> 00:07:42.161
-I had a TV show
that Lorne produced,

00:07:42.194 --> 00:07:43.295
but he won't tell you that.

00:07:43.328 --> 00:07:44.363
It had my name on it.

00:07:44.396 --> 00:07:45.464
Should've said "Michaels."

00:07:45.497 --> 00:07:46.465
No, but it was called "Mulaney"

00:07:46.498 --> 00:07:47.466
and it was canceled.

00:07:47.499 --> 00:07:48.501 line:0%
"Mulaney" was canceled.

00:07:48.534 --> 00:07:50.236 line:0%
The last episode of that show

00:07:50.269 --> 00:07:52.238
aired on Fox against

00:07:52.271 --> 00:07:54.006
the "SNL" 40th anniversary.

00:07:54.039 --> 00:07:57.076
I was at the "SNL" 40th,
not watching my own show.

00:07:57.109 --> 00:08:01.213 line:0%
Lorne asked me to come back
and write the Q&amp;A

00:08:01.246 --> 00:08:02.848 line:0%
that Jerry Seinfeld did
with the audience.

00:08:02.881 --> 00:08:04.517 line:0%
There's a really nice thing
about coming back

00:08:04.550 --> 00:08:05.951
and being like a writer

00:08:05.984 --> 00:08:09.388
who is really excited
to be in the studio.

00:08:09.421 --> 00:08:11.323
Sometimes people were like,
"I don't wanna come back!"

00:08:11.356 --> 00:08:14.894
And then those people would be
there on Friday and Saturday.

00:08:14.927 --> 00:08:18.464
And then, I was here to do
a walk-on part in a "Stefon."

00:08:18.497 --> 00:08:22.701 line:0%
-He's an attorney named Shy.
Shy.

00:08:22.734 --> 00:08:32.378 line:0%
[ Boisterous cheering
and applause ]

00:08:32.411 --> 00:08:33.879
-Hi. Hi, Shy.

00:08:33.912 --> 00:08:35.915
-Hello, gentlemen.
How are ya?

00:08:35.948 --> 00:08:37.182
I was wearing a latex shirt

00:08:37.215 --> 00:08:38.684
and Lorne called me up
to his office.

00:08:38.717 --> 00:08:40.019
Perfect timing.
[ Both chuckle ]

00:08:40.052 --> 00:08:42.655
He and I were sitting there
and he said,

00:08:42.688 --> 00:08:44.757
"I'd like you to host April."

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:46.325
And I turned I looked
at the board

00:08:46.358 --> 00:08:49.161 line:0%
and I went,
"What about Timothée Chalamet?"

00:08:49.194 --> 00:08:51.030
And then I digested
what he was saying

00:08:51.063 --> 00:08:53.132
and so I was really terrified.

00:08:53.165 --> 00:08:55.134
-Ladies and gentlemen,

00:08:55.167 --> 00:08:57.202 line:0%
John Mulaney!

00:08:57.235 --> 00:09:00.039 line:0%
-Thank you. Thank you.
Thank you very much.

00:09:00.072 --> 00:09:04.109 line:0%
It is great to be here,
hosting "Saturday Night Live."

00:09:04.142 --> 00:09:07.746
I was a writer here
for five years,

00:09:07.779 --> 00:09:09.548
some of the best years
of my life,

00:09:09.581 --> 00:09:11.584
and to be hosting here
is just surreal.

00:09:11.617 --> 00:09:12.818
[ Cheering, whistling,
and applause ]

00:09:12.851 --> 00:09:14.086
Stick around.
We'll be right back.

00:09:14.119 --> 00:09:17.156
[ Cheering and applause
continue ]

00:09:17.189 --> 00:09:19.224
-The thing is,
once you work here,

00:09:19.257 --> 00:09:20.826
you always work here.
[ Chuckle ]

00:09:20.859 --> 00:09:22.227
There's great comfort
in being here

00:09:22.260 --> 00:09:23.896
and there's great comfort
in working

00:09:23.929 --> 00:09:27.433
for someone who you respect.

00:09:27.466 --> 00:09:30.569
This has been an extraordinary
thing to be a part of

00:09:30.602 --> 00:09:33.939
and I got to do
a lot of stuff here

00:09:33.972 --> 00:09:37.076
with a lotta people I really
loved and still am close to.

00:09:37.109 --> 00:09:40.112
Like it took about a year
after leaving to be like,

00:09:40.145 --> 00:09:42.081
"Oh, 25 to 29?

00:09:42.114 --> 00:09:44.683
That was a wonderful period."

00:09:44.716 --> 00:09:46.652
I would say, to those of you

00:09:46.685 --> 00:09:48.053
who work here,
who are watching this --

00:09:48.086 --> 00:09:49.521
which, I don't know
how many people would --

00:09:49.554 --> 00:09:50.522
[ Laughter ]

00:09:50.555 --> 00:09:51.624
stay one year longer

00:09:51.657 --> 00:09:54.293
than you think you should.

00:09:54.326 --> 00:09:56.662
Was all that unusable?

